1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,743
Welcome to Project 2.

2
00:00:05,023 --> 00:00:08,569
In this course, you build a real data lake, on your own laptop.

3
00:00:08,849 --> 00:00:14,367
Not a toy: a lake with versions, time travel, maintenance, and dbt models on top.

4
00:00:15,318 --> 00:00:17,683
You need no cloud account and no server.

5
00:00:17,963 --> 00:00:22,492
Everything runs inside Rosetta DBT Studio, with open source tools.

6
00:00:23,442 --> 00:00:30,671
We start from a public bucket on Amazon S3, with the Ethereum blockchain as Parquet files, one folder a day.

7
00:00:31,621 --> 00:00:38,140
We load it into a DuckLake: a catalog database, and a folder of Parquet files that the catalog manages.

8
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:44,917
Then dbt builds clean models on top: staging views, and marts that grow day by day.

9
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:52,647
And at the end, answers: how busy the network was each day, what gas cost, and who sent the most transactions.

10
00:00:53,597 --> 00:00:54,768
The data is real.

11
00:00:55,048 --> 00:01:02,474
Three months of Ethereum blocks: August to October 2026, almost six hundred sixty thousand blocks.

12
00:01:03,424 --> 00:01:07,174
And one week of transactions: twelve point seven million rows.

13
00:01:08,124 --> 00:01:11,103
Big enough to feel real, small enough for a laptop.

14
00:01:11,383 --> 00:01:13,886
About three gigabytes of free disk is plenty.

15
00:01:14,836 --> 00:01:17,822
One app does it all: Rosetta DBT Studio.

16
00:01:18,102 --> 00:01:23,335
It has screens for data lakes, a SQL editor, dbt, and git, in one window.

17
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:28,314
Underneath, DuckDB is the engine, and DuckLake is the lake format.

18
00:01:28,594 --> 00:01:30,540
And dbt builds the models.

19
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:32,461
Here is the route.

20
00:01:32,741 --> 00:01:40,802
First, we install Studio, and explain data lakes: warehouses, lakehouses, Parquet, table formats, and how DuckLake works.

21
00:01:41,752 --> 00:01:42,730
Then hands on.

22
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:49,596
We create the lake and load it, change data safely, make it fast and keep it tidy, and look inside the catalog.

23
00:01:50,546 --> 00:01:53,597
Finally, dbt on the lake, and where to go next.

24
00:01:54,547 --> 00:01:58,059
You need a computer, some basic SQL, and a GitHub account.

25
00:01:58,339 --> 00:02:03,789
Every hands-on lesson ends with a commit to the course repository, linked in the description.

26
00:02:04,069 --> 00:02:05,451
See you in the next lesson.

27
00:02:13,417 --> 00:02:16,630
Rosetta DBT Studio is free and open source.

28
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:20,194
Go to rosetta D B dot I O, and click Download Now.

29
00:02:21,144 --> 00:02:25,989
The download page has a card for each system: Windows, Linux, and macOS.

30
00:02:26,939 --> 00:02:29,295
On Windows, take the E X E installer.

31
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:32,073
On Linux, the deb or R P M package.

32
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:37,081
On a Mac, pick your processor: arm sixty-four for Apple silicon, or Intel.

33
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:40,211
This Mac has an Intel chip, so click Mac Intel.

34
00:02:41,161 --> 00:02:43,401
When the download finishes, open the file.

35
00:02:43,681 --> 00:02:47,857
A window shows the app on the left, and your Applications folder on the right.

36
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:53,462
Drag Rosetta DBT Studio onto Applications, and wait for the copy.

37
00:02:54,412 --> 00:02:56,234
Then open it from Applications.

38
00:02:56,514 --> 00:03:00,926
The first time, macOS asks if you want to open an app from the internet.

39
00:03:01,207 --> 00:03:01,989
Click Open.

40
00:03:02,939 --> 00:03:04,689
Studio starts with a short setup.

41
00:03:04,969 --> 00:03:09,600
First, the dbt version: dbt 1.x, the recommended one.

42
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:11,822
Then the exact version.

43
00:03:12,102 --> 00:03:14,701
Keep the latest, 1.12.6.

44
00:03:15,651 --> 00:03:16,800
Next, the adapters.

45
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:23,775
For this course we only need DuckDB, so uncheck the others, keep dbt duckdb, and click Continue.

46
00:03:24,725 --> 00:03:27,058
Skip the optional tools, and click Install.

47
00:03:28,008 --> 00:03:35,508
Studio downloads its own Python, then installs dbt Core and the DuckDB adapter, inside its own folder.

48
00:03:35,788 --> 00:03:38,359
It takes a minute or two; the video speeds it up.

49
00:03:39,309 --> 00:03:45,102
Done: Python 3.10.17, and dbt 1.12.6.

50
00:03:45,382 --> 00:03:46,140
Click Finish.

51
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:48,623
This is Studio's home screen.

52
00:03:48,903 --> 00:03:52,374
Among the modules is Data Lake: that is where our lake will live.

53
00:03:53,324 --> 00:04:00,461
The status bar shows what was installed: dbt, Python, and the DuckDB version Studio runs inside.

54
00:04:00,741 --> 00:04:03,348
We come back to that one in Lesson 1.6.

55
00:04:04,298 --> 00:04:09,400
To recap: one app, with its own Python and dbt, and DuckDB built in.

56
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,721
You did not install any of them yourself.

57
00:04:12,671 --> 00:04:18,629
Next, before we touch a lake, we explain what a data lake is, starting with warehouses and lakehouses.

58
00:04:26,583 --> 00:04:28,798
Let's start with the classic data warehouse.

59
00:04:29,078 --> 00:04:33,729
One system owns everything: the storage, and the engine that runs your queries.

60
00:04:34,009 --> 00:04:36,552
Data goes in through the warehouse's own door.

61
00:04:37,502 --> 00:04:40,457
That makes it fast and safe, with real transactions.

62
00:04:40,737 --> 00:04:47,794
But the data lives in the warehouse's own format, only its engine can read it, and you pay for storage and compute together.

63
00:04:48,744 --> 00:04:50,405
A data lake turns this around.

64
00:04:50,685 --> 00:04:55,294
The data is just files, on cheap storage: a folder, or a bucket on S3.

65
00:04:55,574 --> 00:04:57,398
In open formats, like Parquet.

66
00:04:58,348 --> 00:05:03,414
Any engine can read those files: DuckDB, Spark, Trino, or a Python script.

67
00:05:03,694 --> 00:05:05,438
Storage and compute are separate.

68
00:05:06,388 --> 00:05:08,066
But plain files have problems.

69
00:05:08,346 --> 00:05:12,283
There are no transactions: a reader can see a write that is only half done.

70
00:05:13,233 --> 00:05:15,206
There are no safe updates or deletes.

71
00:05:15,486 --> 00:05:20,386
To change one row, you rewrite a whole file, and hope nobody reads it at that moment.

72
00:05:21,336 --> 00:05:23,459
And there is no schema and no history.

73
00:05:23,739 --> 00:05:27,436
Which columns does the table have, and what did it look like yesterday?

74
00:05:27,716 --> 00:05:28,946
The files do not say.

75
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:36,040
Even finding the files is slow: listing thousands of objects in a bucket, just to plan one query.

76
00:05:36,990 --> 00:05:40,120
The lakehouse fixes this, without giving up open files.

77
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:47,273
It adds a table format on top: a small layer of metadata that says which files make up each table, right now.

78
00:05:48,223 --> 00:05:56,914
With that layer you get the warehouse features back: transactions, safe updates and deletes, schema changes, and a history you can travel back in.

79
00:05:57,194 --> 00:05:59,003
The data stays in open files.

80
00:05:59,953 --> 00:06:02,035
Four words we use through the whole course.

81
00:06:02,315 --> 00:06:04,771
The lake: the data files, on some storage.

82
00:06:05,721 --> 00:06:11,525
The table format: the rules for keeping track of which files make up a table, and how it changes.

83
00:06:12,475 --> 00:06:15,350
The catalog: where that tracking information is stored.

84
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,875
And the engine: the program that reads it all and runs your queries.

85
00:06:20,155 --> 00:06:22,071
In this course, that is DuckDB.

86
00:06:23,021 --> 00:06:26,156
To recap: a warehouse is one system for everything.

87
00:06:26,436 --> 00:06:27,845
A lake is open files.

88
00:06:28,125 --> 00:06:33,204
A lakehouse is open files, plus a table format that makes them behave like tables.

89
00:06:33,484 --> 00:06:35,775
Next: why lakes store data in Parquet.

90
00:06:43,726 --> 00:06:46,202
A CSV file stores data row by row.

91
00:06:46,482 --> 00:06:50,739
Each line is a whole record: every column of one Ethereum block.

92
00:06:51,689 --> 00:06:53,608
Parquet stores it column by column.

93
00:06:53,888 --> 00:06:58,593
All the block numbers together, then all the miners, then all the transaction counts.

94
00:06:59,543 --> 00:07:00,583
Why does that matter?

95
00:07:00,863 --> 00:07:03,626
Analytics queries use a few columns out of many.

96
00:07:03,906 --> 00:07:07,742
With columns stored apart, the engine reads only the ones it needs.

97
00:07:08,692 --> 00:07:12,950
Here is a real file: one day of Ethereum blocks from the public bucket.

98
00:07:13,230 --> 00:07:18,166
Seven thousand one hundred seventy-six rows, in one row group, compressed with Snappy.

99
00:07:19,116 --> 00:07:22,208
A big file is split into row groups, chunks of rows.

100
00:07:22,488 --> 00:07:25,614
Inside each chunk, every column is stored on its own.

101
00:07:26,564 --> 00:07:28,686
The file is five point six megabytes.

102
00:07:28,966 --> 00:07:32,807
More than half of it is one column, logs bloom, that we do not need.

103
00:07:33,087 --> 00:07:34,727
Later we simply leave it out.

104
00:07:35,677 --> 00:07:42,401
The most useful part: for every column in every row group, the file stores the smallest and the largest value.

105
00:07:42,681 --> 00:07:49,348
Block numbers in this file go from twenty-six million ninety-three thousand to twenty-six million one hundred thousand.

106
00:07:50,298 --> 00:07:59,045
So if you ask for block twenty-six million two hundred thousand, the engine looks at those two numbers and skips the whole file, without reading a single row.

107
00:07:59,995 --> 00:08:07,467
DuckLake goes one step further: it copies these statistics into its catalog, so it can skip files before even opening them.

108
00:08:07,747 --> 00:08:09,645
We see that in Modules 4 and 5.

109
00:08:10,595 --> 00:08:13,423
To recap: Parquet stores columns, not rows.

110
00:08:13,703 --> 00:08:22,510
It splits rows into row groups, compresses each column, and keeps the min and max of every column, so engines can skip what they do not need.

111
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:26,005
Next: how table formats turn these files into tables.

112
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:37,083
Every table format answers the same three questions.

113
00:08:37,363 --> 00:08:39,343
Which files make up the table right now?

114
00:08:39,623 --> 00:08:40,612
What is its schema?

115
00:08:40,892 --> 00:08:43,133
And which version is this, so we can go back?

116
00:08:44,083 --> 00:08:48,542
Apache Iceberg keeps its answers as files, in the same bucket as the data.

117
00:08:48,822 --> 00:08:56,990
A metadata file in JSON points to a manifest list, which points to manifest files, which finally list the Parquet data files.

118
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:03,730
And you still need a catalog service, which holds one pointer: the current metadata file of each table.

119
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:06,681
Delta Lake also uses files.

120
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:14,669
Each change is a numbered JSON file in a folder called delta log, and every so often a checkpoint file sums up the log.

121
00:09:15,619 --> 00:09:18,313
Both designs work, and both are widely used.

122
00:09:18,593 --> 00:09:19,873
But they share some costs.

123
00:09:20,153 --> 00:09:23,006
Every change writes new small metadata files.

124
00:09:23,956 --> 00:09:29,332
A query needs several round trips, file after file, before it reads a single row of data.

125
00:09:30,282 --> 00:09:35,279
And for real use you run a catalog service anyway, often backed by a database.

126
00:09:36,229 --> 00:09:38,094
DuckLake starts from that last point.

127
00:09:38,374 --> 00:09:42,821
If you need a database for the catalog anyway, why not put all the metadata in it?

128
00:09:43,101 --> 00:09:47,365
Files, schemas, snapshots, statistics: all plain SQL tables.

129
00:09:48,315 --> 00:09:53,608
The data itself stays exactly where it was: Parquet files, on a disk or in a bucket.

130
00:09:54,558 --> 00:10:01,075
Now a change is one database transaction, and finding the files for a query is one SQL query.

131
00:10:02,025 --> 00:10:03,040
Side by side.

132
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:09,687
Iceberg and Delta keep metadata as files in the bucket, have many engines, and years of production use.

133
00:10:09,967 --> 00:10:15,031
DuckLake keeps metadata in a database, is read by DuckDB today, and is younger.

134
00:10:15,311 --> 00:10:19,881
In this course, we use DuckLake: it runs on a laptop with no services at all.

135
00:10:20,831 --> 00:10:24,590
To recap: a table format tracks files, schema and versions.

136
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:27,948
Iceberg and Delta store that as files next to the data.

137
00:10:28,228 --> 00:10:30,661
DuckLake stores it as tables in a database.

138
00:10:30,941 --> 00:10:33,302
Next: how DuckLake works, step by step.

139
00:10:41,260 --> 00:10:43,423
A DuckLake has two parts, and only two.

140
00:10:44,373 --> 00:10:46,077
The first part is the catalog.

141
00:10:46,357 --> 00:10:48,243
It is a normal SQL database.

142
00:10:48,523 --> 00:10:53,398
It knows the tables, their columns, and exactly which files belong to each table.

143
00:10:53,678 --> 00:10:58,348
It can be a DuckDB file, SQLite, PostgreSQL, or MySQL.

144
00:10:59,298 --> 00:11:03,813
The second part is the data: a folder, or a bucket, of Parquet files.

145
00:11:04,093 --> 00:11:05,808
These files are never edited.

146
00:11:06,088 --> 00:11:07,856
They are only added, or removed.

147
00:11:08,806 --> 00:11:11,038
Here is what happens when you insert rows.

148
00:11:11,318 --> 00:11:15,710
First, DuckLake writes the new rows as Parquet files in the data folder.

149
00:11:15,990 --> 00:11:17,399
Nobody can see them yet.

150
00:11:18,349 --> 00:11:24,308
Then, in one database transaction, the catalog records those files and creates a new snapshot.

151
00:11:24,588 --> 00:11:27,517
That commit is the moment the change becomes visible.

152
00:11:28,467 --> 00:11:33,651
If anything fails on the way, the catalog does not change, and readers never see half a write.

153
00:11:34,601 --> 00:11:36,071
A read goes the other way.

154
00:11:36,351 --> 00:11:40,507
The query first asks the catalog which files make up the table right now.

155
00:11:41,457 --> 00:11:47,729
The catalog also keeps statistics for every file, like the smallest and largest value of each column.

156
00:11:48,009 --> 00:11:50,567
So files that cannot match the filter are skipped.

157
00:11:51,517 --> 00:11:54,417
Only then does it read the Parquet files that are left.

158
00:11:55,367 --> 00:11:57,318
This simple design gives you a lot.

159
00:11:57,598 --> 00:12:01,251
Changes are real transactions, even across several tables.

160
00:12:02,201 --> 00:12:07,411
Every change is a snapshot, so you can query a table as it was yesterday, or last week.

161
00:12:08,361 --> 00:12:09,860
And a small change stays small.

162
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:14,316
It is a few rows in the catalog, not a pile of new metadata files.

163
00:12:15,266 --> 00:12:16,499
Who can read a DuckLake?

164
00:12:16,779 --> 00:12:19,901
Today, DuckDB does, through the ducklake extension.

165
00:12:20,181 --> 00:12:23,694
That is what Rosetta DBT Studio uses under the hood.

166
00:12:24,644 --> 00:12:30,790
And because the catalog is plain SQL tables, and the data is plain Parquet, nothing is locked away.

167
00:12:31,070 --> 00:12:33,859
Any SQL client can look inside the catalog.

168
00:12:34,809 --> 00:12:38,801
To recap: the catalog is a database, and the data is Parquet.

169
00:12:39,081 --> 00:12:42,087
Files are never edited, only added or removed.

170
00:12:42,367 --> 00:12:44,228
And every change makes a snapshot.

171
00:12:44,508 --> 00:12:48,768
Next, we meet the data for this course: Ethereum blocks and transactions.

172
00:12:56,726 --> 00:12:58,572
Ethereum is a chain of blocks.

173
00:12:58,852 --> 00:13:01,181
A new block is added every twelve seconds.

174
00:13:01,461 --> 00:13:07,186
On the first of October 2026, that was seven thousand one hundred seventy-six blocks.

175
00:13:08,136 --> 00:13:12,895
Each block holds a list of transactions, two hundred eighty-four on average that day.

176
00:13:13,845 --> 00:13:16,742
Every transaction needs some work, measured in gas.

177
00:13:17,022 --> 00:13:21,373
A block has a gas limit, sixty million, and it was about half full on average.

178
00:13:22,323 --> 00:13:27,014
For each unit of gas you pay a base fee, set by the network from how busy it is.

179
00:13:27,294 --> 00:13:29,980
That day it averaged zero point six four gwei.

180
00:13:30,930 --> 00:13:32,988
The units: values are stored in wei.

181
00:13:33,268 --> 00:13:37,278
One ether is ten to the eighteen wei, and one gwei is a billion wei.

182
00:13:37,558 --> 00:13:39,340
Our staging models convert them.

183
00:13:40,290 --> 00:13:44,932
Addresses and hashes are long hexadecimal strings, starting with zero x.

184
00:13:45,212 --> 00:13:46,586
We never read them out loud.

185
00:13:47,536 --> 00:13:49,127
The data is free and public.

186
00:13:49,407 --> 00:13:58,674
Amazon publishes it in the AWS Public Blockchain dataset, as Parquet files on S3, one folder per day, updated every day.

187
00:13:59,624 --> 00:14:00,803
We use two tables.

188
00:14:01,083 --> 00:14:07,245
Blocks: one row per block, with its number, time, the address that built it, the gas used, and the base fee.

189
00:14:08,195 --> 00:14:16,557
And transactions: one row per transaction, with who sent it, to whom, the value, the gas it used, the price paid, and whether it succeeded.

190
00:14:17,507 --> 00:14:21,549
We load three months of blocks: about six hundred sixty thousand rows.

191
00:14:22,499 --> 00:14:28,528
And one week of transactions: twelve point seven million rows, about one point seven gigabytes.

192
00:14:28,808 --> 00:14:33,361
Not every day of the year: just enough to be real, and still fit on a laptop.

193
00:14:41,326 --> 00:14:43,605
Before we build a lake, let's look under the hood.

194
00:14:43,885 --> 00:14:46,807
Rosetta DBT Studio is an Electron app.

195
00:14:47,087 --> 00:14:51,883
That means two parts: the window you see, and a Node.js process behind it.

196
00:14:52,833 --> 00:14:54,905
The window never touches a database.

197
00:14:55,185 --> 00:15:01,386
When you run a query, it sends a request to the main process, which runs the SQL and sends back the rows.

198
00:15:02,336 --> 00:15:08,027
The main process uses DuckDB's official Node package, duck D B slash node A P I.

199
00:15:08,307 --> 00:15:12,498
Studio 1.7.6 ships version 1.5.2.

200
00:15:12,778 --> 00:15:17,757
DuckDB runs inside Studio itself: no server, nothing else to install.

201
00:15:18,707 --> 00:15:20,807
In Studio's code, it is three calls.

202
00:15:21,087 --> 00:15:22,677
Create a DuckDB instance.

203
00:15:22,957 --> 00:15:23,770
Connect to it.

204
00:15:24,050 --> 00:15:25,711
And run SQL on the connection.

205
00:15:26,661 --> 00:15:29,029
DuckLake is not built into DuckDB.

206
00:15:29,309 --> 00:15:30,467
It is an extension.

207
00:15:30,746 --> 00:15:35,422
So on that connection, Studio first runs install ducklake, and load ducklake.

208
00:15:36,372 --> 00:15:40,723
Then it attaches the lake: the catalog file, a name, and the data path.

209
00:15:41,003 --> 00:15:42,934
And it makes the lake the current database.

210
00:15:43,214 --> 00:15:45,454
That is exactly what you could type yourself.

211
00:15:46,404 --> 00:15:48,420
There is one adapter per catalog type.

212
00:15:48,700 --> 00:15:56,263
In 1.7.6 the wizard offers DuckDB; SQLite and PostgreSQL are marked as coming soon.

213
00:15:56,543 --> 00:15:58,921
When a lake is closed, Studio detaches it.

214
00:15:59,871 --> 00:16:01,614
You can see the engine in Studio.

215
00:16:01,894 --> 00:16:03,942
Open Settings, then DuckDB.

216
00:16:04,222 --> 00:16:08,263
This is Studio's own DuckDB database, for caching and previews.

217
00:16:09,213 --> 00:16:14,868
And the status bar always shows the DuckDB version inside the app: 1.5.2.

218
00:16:15,818 --> 00:16:17,120
One more thing for later.

219
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:26,625
When dbt runs, it uses its own DuckDB, the Python package inside dbt duckdb, version 1.5.6.

220
00:16:26,905 --> 00:16:29,348
So two engines read and write the same lake.

221
00:16:29,628 --> 00:16:30,962
We checked that they agree.

222
00:16:31,912 --> 00:16:36,642
But with a DuckDB file as the catalog, only one of them can open it at a time.

223
00:16:36,922 --> 00:16:38,864
Keep that in mind for Module 6.

224
00:16:39,814 --> 00:16:44,096
To recap: DuckDB runs inside Studio, through its Node package.

225
00:16:44,376 --> 00:16:46,730
DuckLake is an extension that Studio loads.

226
00:16:47,010 --> 00:16:50,199
And everything the Data Lake screens do is plain SQL.

227
00:16:50,479 --> 00:16:51,935
Now, let's create our lake.

228
00:16:59,893 --> 00:17:03,872
In this lesson we create the project for the whole course, and our first lake.

229
00:17:04,152 --> 00:17:11,041
On disk, the lake is two things inside one folder: a catalog file, and a data folder for the Parquet files.

230
00:17:11,991 --> 00:17:13,288
We call the lake eth lake.

231
00:17:13,568 --> 00:17:18,263
Its storage is a local folder, and its catalog is a single DuckDB file.

232
00:17:19,213 --> 00:17:26,259
First, the project. dbt needs a connection, so on the home screen click New Connection, and choose DuckDB.

233
00:17:29,406 --> 00:17:34,755
Name it ethereum lake dev, with a small DuckDB file next to the projects folder.

234
00:17:35,035 --> 00:17:38,113
We point dbt at the lake later, in Module 6.

235
00:17:38,393 --> 00:17:38,992
Save.

236
00:17:39,942 --> 00:17:45,394
Now open the DBT Project card, click New Project, and call it ethereum lake.

237
00:17:45,674 --> 00:17:47,202
Pick the connection, and save.

238
00:17:57,543 --> 00:18:01,922
In the project, make a folder called lake, and inside it, a folder called data.

239
00:18:02,202 --> 00:18:03,253
The lake lives here.

240
00:18:10,091 --> 00:18:12,224
Now open Data Lake from the sidebar.

241
00:18:12,504 --> 00:18:13,662
There are no lakes yet.

242
00:18:13,942 --> 00:18:16,289
Click Create DataLake, and choose DuckLake.

243
00:18:20,058 --> 00:18:22,518
Step one, the name: eth underscore lake.

244
00:18:22,798 --> 00:18:23,388
Next.

245
00:18:24,338 --> 00:18:25,588
Step two, storage.

246
00:18:25,868 --> 00:18:29,641
Keep Local, and give the full path of the data folder we just made.

247
00:18:29,921 --> 00:18:31,963
This is where the Parquet files will go.

248
00:18:32,913 --> 00:18:34,360
Step three, the catalog.

249
00:18:34,640 --> 00:18:36,448
DuckDB is the catalog type.

250
00:18:36,728 --> 00:18:42,280
Replace the suggested path with a file in the lake folder: eth catalog dot duckdb.

251
00:18:43,230 --> 00:18:43,820
Next.

252
00:18:44,100 --> 00:18:47,689
The runtime options, memory and threads, can stay as they are.

253
00:18:47,969 --> 00:18:53,041
Next again, and the review shows the name, the data folder, and the DuckDB catalog.

254
00:18:53,321 --> 00:18:54,502
Click Create Instance.

255
00:18:55,452 --> 00:18:56,359
Here is the lake.

256
00:18:56,639 --> 00:18:57,761
No tables yet.

257
00:18:58,041 --> 00:19:01,481
Open History: there is already one snapshot, number zero.

258
00:19:01,761 --> 00:19:03,883
Creating the lake created the schema main.

259
00:19:04,163 --> 00:19:05,836
Every change will add a row here.

260
00:19:06,786 --> 00:19:08,085
Now the SQL Editor.

261
00:19:08,365 --> 00:19:11,385
The connection list has a new group, DuckLake Instances.

262
00:19:11,665 --> 00:19:13,922
Pick eth lake, and ask for its snapshots.

263
00:19:18,210 --> 00:19:19,610
Run it with the green arrow.

264
00:19:19,890 --> 00:19:21,687
One row: snapshot zero.

265
00:19:21,967 --> 00:19:23,284
The lake answers SQL.

266
00:19:24,234 --> 00:19:33,214
In plain DuckDB, the wizard stands for three statements: install the extension, attach the catalog file with its data path, and use it.

267
00:19:33,494 --> 00:19:36,370
Studio runs them each time it opens the lake.

268
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:43,098
And remember: with a DuckDB file as the catalog, one program at a time can open the lake.

269
00:19:43,378 --> 00:19:44,948
For a laptop, that is enough.

270
00:19:45,898 --> 00:19:47,018
Back in the project.

271
00:19:47,298 --> 00:19:54,321
Add a dot gitignore file, so build output, logs, the database files, and the whole lake folder stay out of git.

272
00:19:54,601 --> 00:19:56,319
The lake is data, not code.

273
00:19:58,372 --> 00:19:59,522
And a short README.

274
00:20:10,521 --> 00:20:19,496
On GitHub, create a new, empty, public repository called ethereum ducklake dbt, with no README, gitignore or license.

275
00:20:19,776 --> 00:20:21,327
Here is ours, still empty.

276
00:20:21,607 --> 00:20:23,577
Copy its HTTPS address.

277
00:20:24,527 --> 00:20:28,846
Back in Studio, open the Git tab, and click Initialize Repository.

278
00:20:31,090 --> 00:20:36,234
Stage all the changes, type a message, create the ethereum lake project, and commit.

279
00:20:40,441 --> 00:20:43,881
Click Publish Branch, paste the repository address, and add it.

280
00:20:44,161 --> 00:20:46,400
Studio pushes the main branch to GitHub.

281
00:20:48,025 --> 00:20:49,880
Refresh the repository page.

282
00:20:50,160 --> 00:20:51,603
The project is on GitHub.

283
00:20:51,883 --> 00:20:54,225
The lake folder is not: git ignores it.

284
00:20:55,174 --> 00:21:01,976
To recap: we created the ethereum lake project, and the eth lake DuckLake inside it, with the Data Lake wizard.

285
00:21:02,256 --> 00:21:08,765
It is a catalog file and a data folder, it starts with snapshot zero, and the SQL Editor can query it.

286
00:21:09,045 --> 00:21:10,489
The project is on GitHub.

287
00:21:10,769 --> 00:21:13,416
Next, we load three months of blocks into the lake.

288
00:21:21,366 --> 00:21:24,492
The blocks live in the public bucket, one folder per day.

289
00:21:24,772 --> 00:21:29,909
We read them straight from S3, with read parquet and a wildcard: one month at a time.

290
00:21:30,859 --> 00:21:35,829
Three statements: create the table with August, then insert September, then October.

291
00:21:36,109 --> 00:21:40,266
Each one becomes a snapshot of its own, which we use later for time travel.

292
00:21:41,216 --> 00:21:42,362
One setting first.

293
00:21:42,642 --> 00:21:47,151
DuckLake can keep small writes inside the catalog, which is called data inlining.

294
00:21:47,431 --> 00:21:51,082
We turn it off, so every write becomes Parquet files we can look at.

295
00:21:51,362 --> 00:21:53,214
Lesson 4.3 explains it.

296
00:21:54,164 --> 00:21:57,292
Open the SQL Editor, and pick the eth lake connection.

297
00:21:57,572 --> 00:21:59,928
Here is the script: one statement per line.

298
00:22:03,182 --> 00:22:08,656
Run the first two lines: inlining off, and a schema called raw, for the data as it comes in.

299
00:22:09,606 --> 00:22:13,305
Line three creates raw dot blocks from every file of August.

300
00:22:13,585 --> 00:22:17,742
Star exclude drops two columns we do not need, like the big logs bloom.

301
00:22:18,022 --> 00:22:22,308
Run it: Studio downloads the month from S3 and writes it into the lake.

302
00:22:38,087 --> 00:22:40,659
Then September, and October, with insert into.

303
00:23:34,373 --> 00:23:36,250
Check the result: blocks per month.

304
00:23:36,530 --> 00:23:41,075
About two hundred twenty thousand each, almost six hundred sixty thousand in total.

305
00:23:42,025 --> 00:23:44,776
Now open Data Lake, and the eth lake instance.

306
00:23:45,056 --> 00:23:45,735
Refresh.

307
00:23:46,015 --> 00:23:50,362
The new table is listed: blocks, in schema raw, with its rows and size.

308
00:23:53,008 --> 00:23:57,206
And History has four new snapshots: the schema, then one for each month.

309
00:23:58,156 --> 00:23:59,753
Keep the script in the project.

310
00:24:00,033 --> 00:24:08,891
In DBT Studio, add a folder called sql, and in it a file called 2 underscore 2 load blocks dot sequel, with the same statements.

311
00:24:09,171 --> 00:24:09,770
Save.

312
00:24:14,517 --> 00:24:19,288
Git tab: stage all, commit with load three months of blocks, and Sync Changes to push.

313
00:24:19,568 --> 00:24:23,164
The lake itself stays out of git; the script is enough to rebuild it.

314
00:24:32,157 --> 00:24:39,671
To recap: we turned inlining off, made the raw schema, and loaded three months of blocks from S3, one month per statement.

315
00:24:39,951 --> 00:24:43,832
That is six hundred sixty thousand blocks, and four new snapshots.

316
00:24:44,113 --> 00:24:46,979
Next, we look inside the lake: the files on disk.

317
00:24:54,933 --> 00:24:58,267
Open Data Lake, then eth lake, and click the blocks table.

318
00:24:58,547 --> 00:25:00,744
Studio opens the table's own page.

319
00:25:05,155 --> 00:25:09,526
The Schema tab lists the columns and their types, as the catalog stores them.

320
00:25:10,476 --> 00:25:13,498
And Data Files: three Parquet files, one per load.

321
00:25:13,778 --> 00:25:20,334
August, September, and October, each about seventy megabytes, with its row count and the snapshot that added it.

322
00:25:21,284 --> 00:25:23,570
The same list is one function in SQL.

323
00:25:23,850 --> 00:25:29,927
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, ducklake list files gives each data file of the table, and its size.

324
00:25:33,318 --> 00:25:34,655
The files are on your disk.

325
00:25:34,935 --> 00:25:41,526
Glob lists every Parquet file under the lake's data folder: the same three, in a folder per schema and table.

326
00:25:42,476 --> 00:25:43,849
And they are plain Parquet.

327
00:25:44,129 --> 00:25:49,321
Read them directly, without the lake, and you get the same six hundred sixty thousand rows.

328
00:25:50,271 --> 00:25:53,346
So any tool that reads Parquet can read these files.

329
00:25:54,296 --> 00:25:57,854
But only the catalog knows which files make up the table right now.

330
00:25:58,134 --> 00:26:04,396
After a delete or an update, the folder also holds files that are no longer current, and delete files.

331
00:26:04,676 --> 00:26:07,598
Reading the folder directly would give the wrong answer.

332
00:26:07,878 --> 00:26:09,613
That is what the catalog is for.

333
00:26:10,563 --> 00:26:16,691
Save the queries as sql, 2 underscore 3 look inside dot sequel, then commit, and push.

334
00:26:38,136 --> 00:26:42,321
To recap: Studio's table page shows the schema and the data files.

335
00:26:42,601 --> 00:26:48,034
In SQL, ducklake list files gives the same list, and the files are plain Parquet on disk.

336
00:26:48,314 --> 00:26:51,003
But the catalog decides which files are the table.

337
00:26:51,283 --> 00:26:52,547
Next: snapshots.

338
00:27:00,499 --> 00:27:05,168
Every change to a DuckLake creates a snapshot: a numbered version of the whole lake.

339
00:27:05,448 --> 00:27:07,767
So far we have five: zero to four.

340
00:27:08,717 --> 00:27:10,899
A snapshot is not a copy of the data.

341
00:27:11,179 --> 00:27:15,807
It is a few rows in the catalog that say which files were valid from that moment on.

342
00:27:16,757 --> 00:27:19,725
In Data Lake, open eth lake, and its History tab.

343
00:27:20,005 --> 00:27:24,465
Each row is a snapshot: its number, time, schema version, and what changed.

344
00:27:27,050 --> 00:27:31,004
In the SQL Editor, the same list comes from eth lake dot snapshots.

345
00:27:31,284 --> 00:27:33,996
Changes says, in words, what each snapshot did.

346
00:27:38,989 --> 00:27:42,427
Current snapshot tells you where the lake is now: snapshot four.

347
00:27:43,377 --> 00:27:44,487
Now change something.

348
00:27:44,767 --> 00:27:49,205
Insert one more day of blocks, the first of November, straight from S3.

349
00:27:50,155 --> 00:27:51,904
And list the newest snapshots again.

350
00:27:52,184 --> 00:27:55,063
Snapshot five: inserted into the blocks table.

351
00:27:55,343 --> 00:27:57,055
One statement, one snapshot.

352
00:27:58,005 --> 00:28:04,309
You can also group several statements into one snapshot, with a transaction: begin, your changes, commit.

353
00:28:04,589 --> 00:28:09,106
Inside it, set commit message adds an author and a message, like a git commit.

354
00:28:10,056 --> 00:28:15,307
Studio's SQL Editor runs one statement at a time, so we cannot type a transaction there.

355
00:28:15,587 --> 00:28:20,272
We set a real commit message later, from dbt, in Lesson 6.3.

356
00:28:21,222 --> 00:28:26,147
Save the queries as 2 underscore 4 snapshots dot sequel, commit, and push.

357
00:28:49,079 --> 00:28:51,374
To recap: every change is a snapshot.

358
00:28:51,654 --> 00:28:59,275
Studio shows them in History, SQL shows them with snapshots and current snapshot, and a transaction groups changes into one.

359
00:28:59,555 --> 00:29:02,324
Next: we use snapshots to travel back in time.

360
00:29:10,274 --> 00:29:18,616
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, start with the table as it is now: six hundred sixty-seven thousand blocks, three months and one day.

361
00:29:22,308 --> 00:29:24,190
Now add at, version equals two.

362
00:29:24,470 --> 00:29:31,098
That is the table as it was at snapshot two, right after we loaded August: two hundred twenty-two thousand blocks.

363
00:29:32,048 --> 00:29:33,687
Any query works on the past.

364
00:29:33,967 --> 00:29:38,416
Blocks per month at version three: August and September, no October yet.

365
00:29:39,366 --> 00:29:41,970
Time travel also helps you recover data.

366
00:29:42,250 --> 00:29:48,166
Create a table from the past: October, as it was at version four, before the November day came in.

367
00:29:49,116 --> 00:29:54,356
It holds exactly the October blocks: two hundred twenty-two thousand four hundred fifty-one.

368
00:29:54,636 --> 00:29:57,279
We only needed it for the demo, so drop it again.

369
00:29:58,229 --> 00:30:02,976
You can also travel by time: at timestamp, for example now minus one day.

370
00:30:03,926 --> 00:30:07,650
Or attach the whole lake, read only, as it was at one snapshot.

371
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:12,963
The cost: as long as an old snapshot exists, its files stay on disk.

372
00:30:13,243 --> 00:30:17,000
In Lesson 4.4 we expire old snapshots, and clean up.

373
00:30:17,950 --> 00:30:23,057
Save the queries as 2 underscore 5 time travel dot sequel, commit, and push.

374
00:30:44,885 --> 00:30:49,106
To recap: add at version to any table to read the past, in any query.

375
00:30:49,386 --> 00:30:51,859
Copy past data back with create table as.

376
00:30:52,139 --> 00:30:53,713
That is the end of Module 2.

377
00:30:53,993 --> 00:30:59,110
Next, in Module 3, we change data: updates, deletes, and what they do to the files.

378
00:31:07,074 --> 00:31:09,533
Parquet files cannot be edited in place.

379
00:31:09,813 --> 00:31:12,096
So how does a lake delete or update a row?

380
00:31:12,376 --> 00:31:14,327
Let's watch it happen on a small table.

381
00:31:15,277 --> 00:31:23,528
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, make a working copy: the blocks of the first of October, seven thousand one hundred seventy-six rows.

382
00:31:28,811 --> 00:31:32,007
Seven of those blocks hold fewer than fifty transactions.

383
00:31:32,957 --> 00:31:34,056
Delete those seven.

384
00:31:34,336 --> 00:31:37,829
Then update every hundredth block of the day, seventy-two of them.

385
00:31:38,109 --> 00:31:40,378
Studio just reports that the command ran.

386
00:31:41,328 --> 00:31:42,898
Now list the table's files.

387
00:31:43,178 --> 00:31:47,864
The original data file is still there, untouched, but it now has a delete file.

388
00:31:48,144 --> 00:31:52,692
And there is a second data file: the seventy-two updated rows, written fresh.

389
00:31:53,642 --> 00:31:55,377
The delete file is Parquet too.

390
00:31:55,657 --> 00:31:59,373
It lists row positions in the data file: rows the reader must skip.

391
00:32:00,323 --> 00:32:03,821
In Data Lake, open eth lake, refresh, and click blocks day.

392
00:32:04,101 --> 00:32:06,362
Data Files shows the same two files.

393
00:32:17,020 --> 00:32:20,921
So, a delete writes a small delete file, with the positions to skip.

394
00:32:21,871 --> 00:32:27,259
An update is a delete of the old rows, plus an insert of the new ones in a new data file.

395
00:32:28,209 --> 00:32:33,316
There is also a more compact format, deletion vectors, that you can switch on per table.

396
00:32:33,596 --> 00:32:38,750
And over time, maintenance rewrites files with many deletes, in Lesson 4.4.

397
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:44,653
Save the script as 3 underscore 1 update delete dot sequel, commit, and push.

398
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:11,066
To recap: the lake never edits a Parquet file.

399
00:33:11,346 --> 00:33:15,501
A delete adds a delete file, and an update is a delete plus an insert.

400
00:33:15,781 --> 00:33:19,725
Next: a daily load that can run twice without making duplicates.

401
00:33:27,675 --> 00:33:33,447
Real pipelines run every day, and sometimes twice: a retry after an error, or a click too many.

402
00:33:33,727 --> 00:33:36,227
A plain insert would load the same day twice.

403
00:33:37,177 --> 00:33:41,424
We want an idempotent load: one you can run again, and nothing changes.

404
00:33:42,374 --> 00:33:50,679
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: insert the blocks of the second of November, but only those where no block with the same number exists yet.

405
00:33:54,916 --> 00:33:55,815
Count by day.

406
00:33:56,095 --> 00:34:07,576
Studio 1.7.6 shows a date column as a day number, so read the rows in order: the first and the second of November, seven thousand one hundred seventy-six blocks each.

407
00:34:08,526 --> 00:34:11,259
Now run the load again, as if a job retried.

408
00:34:12,209 --> 00:34:15,553
Count again: still seven thousand one hundred seventy-six.

409
00:34:15,833 --> 00:34:16,898
No duplicates.

410
00:34:17,848 --> 00:34:20,915
And the snapshots: one new snapshot for the first run.

411
00:34:21,195 --> 00:34:25,025
The second run inserted nothing, so it did not even create a snapshot.

412
00:34:25,974 --> 00:34:27,796
The general tool is merge into.

413
00:34:28,076 --> 00:34:33,882
It matches new rows to existing ones on a key, updates the rows that match, and inserts the rest.

414
00:34:34,162 --> 00:34:35,371
DuckLake supports it.

415
00:34:36,321 --> 00:34:41,551
Studio's SQL Editor does not accept merge, so here we used insert where not exists.

416
00:34:41,831 --> 00:34:46,365
In Lesson 6.3, dbt runs a real merge on this lake for us.

417
00:34:47,315 --> 00:34:52,018
Save the load as 3 underscore 2 daily load dot sequel, commit, and push.

418
00:35:15,456 --> 00:35:20,417
To recap: insert only rows whose key is new, and the load can run twice safely.

419
00:35:20,697 --> 00:35:23,399
A change that changes nothing makes no snapshot.

420
00:35:23,679 --> 00:35:25,875
And merge does the same, with updates too.

421
00:35:26,155 --> 00:35:28,218
Next: changing a table's columns.

422
00:35:36,175 --> 00:35:41,190
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: add a column to blocks, gas used percent, a double.

423
00:35:48,209 --> 00:35:50,595
It is instant: no file is rewritten.

424
00:35:50,875 --> 00:35:52,798
Old rows simply have no value.

425
00:35:53,078 --> 00:35:57,957
Fill it for one day, the second of November: gas used divided by the gas limit.

426
00:35:58,907 --> 00:36:02,644
Miner is an old name; today the field means the fee recipient.

427
00:36:02,924 --> 00:36:03,628
Rename it.

428
00:36:04,578 --> 00:36:06,684
And make number required: not null.

429
00:36:08,189 --> 00:36:12,214
Query the new shape: number, fee recipient, and gas used percent.

430
00:36:13,164 --> 00:36:16,574
Now time travel to version eleven, before these changes.

431
00:36:16,854 --> 00:36:18,735
The column is still called miner there.

432
00:36:19,015 --> 00:36:20,842
Each snapshot keeps its own schema.

433
00:36:21,792 --> 00:36:28,995
In Data Lake, open the blocks table and its Schema tab: fee recipient in place of miner, and the new column at the end.

434
00:36:38,071 --> 00:36:40,149
How does this work without touching files?

435
00:36:40,429 --> 00:36:42,926
Every column has an id in the catalog.

436
00:36:43,206 --> 00:36:48,307
A rename changes the name, not the id, so old files still map to the right column.

437
00:36:49,257 --> 00:36:56,153
You can add, drop and rename columns, promote types the safe way, like integer to big integer, and add not null.

438
00:36:56,433 --> 00:36:58,429
And every version keeps its own schema.

439
00:36:59,379 --> 00:37:04,643
Save the script as 3 underscore 3 schema evolution dot sequel, commit, and push.

440
00:37:27,728 --> 00:37:33,734
To recap: we added a column, renamed one, and added a constraint, without rewriting a single file.

441
00:37:34,014 --> 00:37:36,292
And time travel still sees the old schema.

442
00:37:36,572 --> 00:37:39,677
Next: asking the lake what changed between two versions.

443
00:37:47,642 --> 00:37:49,295
Remember Lesson 3.1?

444
00:37:49,575 --> 00:37:55,405
We deleted seven rows of blocks day at snapshot nine, and updated seventy-two at snapshot ten.

445
00:37:55,685 --> 00:37:58,389
Ask the lake what changed between those two snapshots.

446
00:37:59,339 --> 00:38:03,884
Ducklake table changes, for schema raw, table blocks day, from nine to ten.

447
00:38:04,164 --> 00:38:15,097
Grouped by type, Studio's DuckDB, version 1.5.2, shows the update as a delete plus an insert: seventy-nine deletes, and seventy-two inserts at ten.

448
00:38:16,047 --> 00:38:24,026
Look at the rows: each updated block appears twice, the old value in lower case, deleted, and the new value in upper case, inserted.

449
00:38:24,306 --> 00:38:34,268
Newer DuckDB, like the 1.5.6 inside dbt, labels these as update pre-image and post-image, and puts both at snapshot ten.

450
00:38:34,549 --> 00:38:37,090
Change feeds are young: check them on your version.

451
00:38:38,040 --> 00:38:47,599
And two shortcuts: table insertions, seven thousand one hundred seventy-six rows at snapshot eight, and table deletions, seven at snapshot nine.

452
00:38:48,549 --> 00:38:53,578
Four change types: insert, delete, and for updates, the pre-image and the post-image.

453
00:38:54,528 --> 00:38:59,721
This works because every row in a DuckLake has a stable row id, kept across updates.

454
00:39:00,001 --> 00:39:01,455
That is called row lineage.

455
00:39:02,405 --> 00:39:03,289
What is it for?

456
00:39:03,569 --> 00:39:08,937
Downstream jobs that only process what changed since their last run, instead of everything.

457
00:39:09,887 --> 00:39:14,965
Save the queries as 3 underscore 4 change feed dot sequel, commit, and push.

458
00:39:37,467 --> 00:39:45,219
To recap: ducklake table changes tells you every row that changed between two snapshots, with before and after values.

459
00:39:45,499 --> 00:39:48,770
Next: transactions, and what happens with two writers.

460
00:39:56,720 --> 00:39:58,564
A transaction groups changes.

461
00:39:58,844 --> 00:40:03,044
Here, a delete in one table and a new table, between begin and commit.

462
00:40:03,324 --> 00:40:06,586
The lake records both in one snapshot, or nothing at all.

463
00:40:07,536 --> 00:40:15,155
Change commit to rollback, and it is as if nothing happened: no files are added to the table, and no snapshot is created.

464
00:40:16,105 --> 00:40:20,093
In Studio's SQL Editor, every statement is its own transaction.

465
00:40:20,373 --> 00:40:23,139
Try to start one by hand: begin transaction.

466
00:40:26,409 --> 00:40:29,830
Studio refuses it, and lists the statement types it accepts.

467
00:40:30,110 --> 00:40:36,099
To group changes, use a SQL client that keeps one session open, or a tool like dbt.

468
00:40:37,049 --> 00:40:38,942
What about two writers at the same time?

469
00:40:39,222 --> 00:40:40,738
DuckLake is optimistic.

470
00:40:41,018 --> 00:40:42,448
Both work without locking.

471
00:40:42,728 --> 00:40:46,933
When the second one commits, it is checked against what the first one already committed.

472
00:40:47,883 --> 00:40:53,212
Two inserts into the same table do not conflict, and neither do changes to different tables.

473
00:40:53,492 --> 00:40:55,753
DuckLake simply retries the commit on top.

474
00:40:56,703 --> 00:41:05,704
But if both delete rows from the same data file, or both change the same table's schema, the second commit fails, and that writer must try again.

475
00:41:06,654 --> 00:41:11,314
All of this needs a catalog that many programs can open, like PostgreSQL.

476
00:41:11,594 --> 00:41:17,617
Our catalog is a DuckDB file, and a DuckDB file can be opened by one program at a time.

477
00:41:18,567 --> 00:41:24,037
We will see that in Module 6: while Studio has the lake open, dbt cannot open it.

478
00:41:24,317 --> 00:41:27,763
Fine on a laptop, but a shared lake needs a shared catalog.

479
00:41:28,713 --> 00:41:32,392
To recap: a transaction is all or nothing, and one snapshot.

480
00:41:32,672 --> 00:41:36,668
In Studio's SQL Editor every statement is its own transaction.

481
00:41:36,948 --> 00:41:40,022
Appends do not conflict, deletes in the same file do.

482
00:41:40,302 --> 00:41:43,865
And a DuckDB catalog file means one program at a time.

483
00:41:44,145 --> 00:41:45,517
That ends Module 3.

484
00:41:45,797 --> 00:41:48,272
Next: performance, starting with partitioning.

485
00:41:56,222 --> 00:42:00,011
Transactions are big: almost thirteen million rows for one week.

486
00:42:00,291 --> 00:42:02,633
And most questions ask about a day or two.

487
00:42:03,583 --> 00:42:05,481
So we partition the table by date.

488
00:42:05,761 --> 00:42:11,478
Every day gets its own folder of files, and a query for one day only opens that day's files.

489
00:42:12,428 --> 00:42:17,843
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: create raw dot transactions, with the columns we need.

490
00:42:18,124 --> 00:42:20,372
We leave out input, the largest column.

491
00:42:20,652 --> 00:42:24,004
Then set it partitioned by date, before any data goes in.

492
00:42:27,373 --> 00:42:31,451
Now load the week, the first to the seventh of October, straight from S3.

493
00:42:31,731 --> 00:42:35,994
That is about nine hundred megabytes to download, so it takes a few minutes.

494
00:42:36,274 --> 00:42:37,524
The video speeds it up.

495
00:47:13,865 --> 00:47:17,051
Transactions per day: between one and a half and two million.

496
00:47:17,331 --> 00:47:19,294
Twelve point seven million in total.

497
00:47:20,244 --> 00:47:26,406
On disk, under raw transactions, there is now one folder per date, with one Parquet file in each.

498
00:47:27,356 --> 00:47:32,235
A query for one day, the third of October, only needs the file in that day's folder.

499
00:47:32,515 --> 00:47:36,764
The catalog knows which file holds which date, so the rest is never opened.

500
00:47:37,714 --> 00:47:40,378
In Data Lake, open the transactions table.

501
00:47:40,658 --> 00:47:42,790
The Partitions tab shows the key: date.

502
00:47:54,475 --> 00:47:59,225
Choose a key that your queries filter on, with not too many values: a day, or a month.

503
00:47:59,505 --> 00:48:03,723
Partition by something like an address, and you get millions of tiny files.

504
00:48:04,673 --> 00:48:06,129
You can change the key later.

505
00:48:06,409 --> 00:48:10,638
The new key applies to new data only; existing files stay as they are.

506
00:48:11,588 --> 00:48:16,366
Save the script as 4 underscore 1 partitioning dot sequel, commit, and push.

507
00:48:39,623 --> 00:48:47,160
To recap: raw transactions is partitioned by date, with one folder per day, so a query for one day opens one file.

508
00:48:47,440 --> 00:48:51,586
Next: statistics, and how they skip files even without partitions.

509
00:48:59,536 --> 00:49:03,635
In Data Lake, open the transactions table and its Statistics tab.

510
00:49:03,915 --> 00:49:08,007
For every column, the catalog keeps the smallest and the largest value.

511
00:49:08,287 --> 00:49:15,150
Block numbers go from about twenty-six million ninety-three thousand to twenty-six million one hundred forty-four thousand.

512
00:49:16,100 --> 00:49:21,919
The catalog keeps the same numbers for every single file, so it can rule out files without opening them.

513
00:49:22,869 --> 00:49:27,087
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: the first and last block number of the week.

514
00:49:34,751 --> 00:49:38,269
Now ask for a hundred blocks, with explain analyze in front.

515
00:49:38,549 --> 00:49:48,483
It runs the query and returns the plan as text; Studio puts it on one line, so here is the part that matters, from the table scan: total files read, one.

516
00:49:48,763 --> 00:49:52,633
Six of the seven files were skipped, using only their statistics.

517
00:49:53,583 --> 00:49:56,355
Pruning works when each file covers a narrow range.

518
00:49:56,635 --> 00:50:00,833
Here it does: block numbers grow with time, and each day is its own file.

519
00:50:01,783 --> 00:50:07,730
If rows arrive in random order, every file covers almost everything, and nothing can be skipped.

520
00:50:08,680 --> 00:50:13,963
Sorted by tells DuckLake to sort rows when it writes new files, so their ranges stay narrow.

521
00:50:14,913 --> 00:50:17,717
Set it for transactions: sorted by block number.

522
00:50:17,997 --> 00:50:21,932
It applies to new writes, and to files that maintenance rewrites later.

523
00:50:22,882 --> 00:50:27,566
Other settings live in the lake's options, like the inlining limit we set to zero.

524
00:50:27,846 --> 00:50:32,461
Others, like target file size and Parquet compression, are set with set option.

525
00:50:33,411 --> 00:50:38,107
Save the script as 4 underscore 2 statistics dot sequel, commit, and push.

526
00:51:01,907 --> 00:51:09,177
To recap: the catalog keeps min and max for every column of every file, so queries skip files without opening them.

527
00:51:09,457 --> 00:51:11,288
Sorted by keeps ranges narrow.

528
00:51:11,568 --> 00:51:14,257
Next: what DuckLake does with very small writes.

529
00:51:22,207 --> 00:51:28,598
Lakes have a classic problem: many small inserts, like a stream of events, make many tiny Parquet files.

530
00:51:28,878 --> 00:51:31,542
Tiny files are slow to list and slow to read.

531
00:51:32,492 --> 00:51:34,658
DuckLake's answer is data inlining.

532
00:51:34,938 --> 00:51:38,857
Small inserts are stored as rows inside the catalog database.

533
00:51:39,137 --> 00:51:42,136
Later, they are flushed into one proper Parquet file.

534
00:51:43,086 --> 00:51:48,453
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: a small table, a watchlist of addresses with a label.

535
00:51:48,733 --> 00:51:51,164
Remember, inlining is off for the whole lake.

536
00:51:51,444 --> 00:51:55,779
Turn it on for this one table: up to a hundred rows stay in the catalog.

537
00:51:58,031 --> 00:52:02,231
Insert two addresses: the staking deposit contract, and wrapped ether.

538
00:52:02,511 --> 00:52:04,373
And read them back, like any table.

539
00:52:05,323 --> 00:52:07,737
But count the table's data files: zero.

540
00:52:08,017 --> 00:52:10,211
The two rows live inside the catalog.

541
00:52:11,161 --> 00:52:15,833
Flush the inlined data, and count again: one Parquet file, with both rows.

542
00:52:17,681 --> 00:52:20,334
Inlining is on by default for small inserts.

543
00:52:20,614 --> 00:52:25,386
We turned it off in Lesson 2.2 only so that every write would show up as files.

544
00:52:26,336 --> 00:52:32,793
Keep it on for frequent small writes, and flush on a schedule, or let maintenance do it, which is the next lesson.

545
00:52:33,743 --> 00:52:38,421
Save the script as 4 underscore 3 inlining dot sequel, commit, and push.

546
00:53:01,168 --> 00:53:09,871
To recap: with inlining, small inserts become rows in the catalog, not tiny files, and a flush writes them out as one Parquet file.

547
00:53:10,151 --> 00:53:13,146
Next: maintenance, to keep the lake small and fast.

548
00:53:21,107 --> 00:53:22,511
A lake grows untidy.

549
00:53:22,791 --> 00:53:28,391
Every load adds files, old files stay around for time travel, and delete files pile up.

550
00:53:29,341 --> 00:53:37,526
DuckLake's recommended routine has three steps: merge small files, expire old snapshots, and clean up files nobody needs.

551
00:53:37,806 --> 00:53:39,535
Checkpoint runs them all together.

552
00:53:40,485 --> 00:53:44,400
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: count the Parquet files on disk.

553
00:53:44,680 --> 00:53:45,389
Nineteen.

554
00:53:52,519 --> 00:53:53,955
Merge adjacent files.

555
00:53:54,235 --> 00:54:03,014
Studio shows no result rows for this call, but behind it, the four files of the blocks table, the three months and the November days, became one.

556
00:54:03,964 --> 00:54:05,251
Count again: twenty.

557
00:54:05,531 --> 00:54:10,284
One more, not fewer: the new file is written, and the four old ones are still on disk.

558
00:54:11,234 --> 00:54:16,822
Snapshots six and seven are the October copy we made and dropped in Lesson 2.5.

559
00:54:17,102 --> 00:54:18,682
We will never travel back to them.

560
00:54:18,962 --> 00:54:19,829
Expire them.

561
00:54:20,109 --> 00:54:22,853
Again Studio shows no rows, but they are gone.

562
00:54:23,803 --> 00:54:25,259
Now clean up old files.

563
00:54:25,539 --> 00:54:33,120
First a dry run, which only lists what it would delete: the files of that October copy, and the files the merge replaced.

564
00:54:34,070 --> 00:54:36,667
Run it for real, and count: fourteen files.

565
00:54:36,947 --> 00:54:41,185
And time travel still works: the merged file keeps every row's history.

566
00:54:42,135 --> 00:54:44,898
In practice, you just run checkpoint, now and then.

567
00:54:45,178 --> 00:54:50,711
It flushes inlined data, merges, expires and cleans up, following the lake's options.

568
00:54:51,661 --> 00:54:53,058
Be careful with two things.

569
00:54:53,338 --> 00:54:56,451
Expiring a snapshot ends time travel to it, for good.

570
00:54:56,731 --> 00:54:58,432
And cleanup really deletes files.

571
00:54:59,382 --> 00:55:07,150
Files that no snapshot ever knew about, for example after a crash in the middle of a write, are removed with delete orphaned files.

572
00:55:08,100 --> 00:55:12,853
Save the script as 4 underscore 4 maintenance dot sequel, commit, and push.

573
00:55:35,550 --> 00:55:41,904
To recap: merge small files, expire snapshots you do not need, clean up, or simply run checkpoint.

574
00:55:42,184 --> 00:55:46,686
Next: registering Parquet files that already exist, without copying them.

575
00:55:54,640 --> 00:56:00,946
Sometimes the Parquet files already exist: another tool wrote them, or you are moving an old data lake.

576
00:56:01,226 --> 00:56:05,533
Instead of reading and writing them again, you can register them in the catalog.

577
00:56:06,483 --> 00:56:10,336
In the project, inside the lake folder, make a folder called landing.

578
00:56:10,616 --> 00:56:12,404
Git ignores everything under lake.

579
00:56:17,096 --> 00:56:24,775
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: copy one day of blocks from S3, the third of November, into a Parquet file in landing.

580
00:56:25,055 --> 00:56:30,400
We give it the table's current shape: fee recipient instead of miner, and gas used percent.

581
00:56:31,350 --> 00:56:35,720
Now ducklake add data files: the lake, the table, the file, and the schema.

582
00:56:36,000 --> 00:56:39,265
Nothing is copied: the catalog just records the file.

583
00:56:39,545 --> 00:56:46,539
One Studio detail: the result pane runs a statement again to show its rows, which would register the file twice.

584
00:56:46,819 --> 00:56:50,117
So we wrap the call in create temp table, which runs once.

585
00:56:51,067 --> 00:56:56,249
Blocks per day: the third of November is there, seven thousand one hundred eighty-two blocks.

586
00:56:57,199 --> 00:57:01,402
And the table's file list now includes the landing file, at its own path.

587
00:57:02,352 --> 00:57:05,711
One thing to know: from now on, the file belongs to the lake.

588
00:57:05,991 --> 00:57:08,133
Maintenance can merge it, and clean it up.

589
00:57:08,413 --> 00:57:10,227
Do not keep using it from outside.

590
00:57:11,177 --> 00:57:21,015
If a file has a different shape, two options help: allow missing for columns the file does not have, and ignore extra columns for columns the table does not have.

591
00:57:21,965 --> 00:57:28,233
Save the script as 4 underscore 5 add files dot sequel in the sql folder, commit, and push.

592
00:57:46,643 --> 00:57:54,329
To recap: ducklake add data files registers an existing Parquet file without copying it, and from then on the lake owns it.

593
00:57:54,609 --> 00:57:55,954
That ends Module 4.

594
00:57:56,234 --> 00:57:58,170
Next, we open the catalog itself.

595
00:58:06,120 --> 00:58:11,016
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, list the databases this connection has attached.

596
00:58:11,296 --> 00:58:19,910
Two matter: eth lake, the lake, and underscore underscore ducklake metadata eth lake: the catalog file, as a plain DuckDB database.

597
00:58:20,860 --> 00:58:22,883
Its tables all start with ducklake.

598
00:58:23,163 --> 00:58:26,226
These are the tables the DuckLake specification defines.

599
00:58:26,506 --> 00:58:29,271
Nothing else: the whole lake's metadata is in them.

600
00:58:30,221 --> 00:58:34,132
Ducklake snapshot has one row per snapshot, with its schema version.

601
00:58:34,412 --> 00:58:38,444
Six and seven are missing: we expired them in Lesson 4.4.

602
00:58:39,394 --> 00:58:46,360
Ducklake snapshot changes says what each snapshot did: the November file we added, the merge, the inlining flush.

603
00:58:46,640 --> 00:58:48,798
This is what Studio's History tab shows.

604
00:58:49,078 --> 00:58:51,267
A commit message would be stored here too.

605
00:58:52,217 --> 00:58:57,307
Ducklake table: one row per table, with an id, and the snapshot it was created in.

606
00:58:57,587 --> 00:59:02,302
Begin snapshot, end snapshot: a table that was dropped gets an end snapshot.

607
00:59:03,252 --> 00:59:06,391
And ducklake column, for column three of the blocks table.

608
00:59:06,671 --> 00:59:12,939
Two rows: miner, valid from snapshot two until fourteen, and fee recipient from fourteen on.

609
00:59:13,219 --> 00:59:15,500
That is the rename from Lesson 3.3.

610
00:59:15,780 --> 00:59:19,475
Same id, new name, and the old name is kept for time travel.

611
00:59:20,425 --> 00:59:22,454
That is the whole trick of the catalog.

612
00:59:22,734 --> 00:59:25,870
Every row has a begin snapshot and an end snapshot.

613
00:59:26,150 --> 00:59:32,617
The lake at snapshot s is simply every row where begin is at most s, and end is after s, or empty.

614
00:59:33,567 --> 00:59:39,091
Save the queries as 5 underscore 1 catalog tables dot sequel, commit, and push.

615
01:00:01,540 --> 01:00:05,452
To recap: the catalog is a plain database of ducklake tables.

616
01:00:05,732 --> 01:00:11,176
Snapshots, tables and columns are rows, and begin and end snapshot version all of them.

617
01:00:11,456 --> 01:00:14,472
Next: the files, their statistics, and partitions.

618
01:00:22,422 --> 01:00:24,317
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake.

619
01:00:24,597 --> 01:00:27,595
Ducklake data file has one row per Parquet file.

620
01:00:27,875 --> 01:00:36,238
Here are the files of transactions, table five: one per day, with its row count, its size, and its path, relative to the data folder.

621
01:00:37,188 --> 01:00:42,331
Ducklake file column stats holds the min and max of every column of every file.

622
01:00:42,611 --> 01:00:47,484
For block number, each file covers its own narrow range, one day after the other.

623
01:00:47,764 --> 01:00:52,699
This is exactly what let the query in Lesson 4.2 skip six files out of seven.

624
01:00:53,649 --> 01:01:00,415
Ducklake file partition value: the date each file belongs to, from the partitioning in Lesson 4.1.

625
01:01:01,365 --> 01:01:06,513
Ducklake table stats keeps totals per table, so counting rows needs no file at all.

626
01:01:06,793 --> 01:01:11,509
Transactions: twelve point seven million rows, one point seven gigabytes.

627
01:01:12,459 --> 01:01:21,796
And ducklake inlined data tables: the watchlist from Lesson 4.3 has a table inside the catalog, where inlined rows wait to be flushed.

628
01:01:22,746 --> 01:01:34,382
So these are the file tables: data files and delete files, statistics per file, partition values, table totals, inlined rows, and files scheduled for deletion by maintenance.

629
01:01:35,332 --> 01:01:40,566
Save the queries as 5 underscore 2 catalog files dot sequel, commit, and push.

630
01:02:03,327 --> 01:02:09,342
To recap: every file is a row, with its statistics and partition value, and tables keep totals.

631
01:02:09,622 --> 01:02:15,324
Next, the final proof: we read the lake by hand, with nothing but these tables and read parquet.

632
01:02:23,289 --> 01:02:26,627
To end the catalog module, let's read the lake without the lake.

633
01:02:26,907 --> 01:02:36,801
The DuckLake specification describes the steps: find the current snapshot, find the table, find the data path, list the files valid at that snapshot, and read them.

634
01:02:37,751 --> 01:02:39,646
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake.

635
01:02:39,926 --> 01:02:43,592
Step one: the current snapshot is the largest snapshot id.

636
01:02:43,872 --> 01:02:44,659
Twenty-four.

637
01:02:49,785 --> 01:02:52,436
Step two: the transactions table, valid now.

638
01:02:52,716 --> 01:02:55,649
Table id five, and its folder, transactions.

639
01:02:56,599 --> 01:03:01,005
Step three: the lake's data path, from ducklake metadata: our data folder.

640
01:03:01,285 --> 01:03:05,743
Each file's path is relative to it, under the schema and the table's folder.

641
01:03:06,693 --> 01:03:16,429
Step four: the data files of table five that are valid at snapshot twenty-four: begin snapshot at most twenty-four, end snapshot after it, or empty.

642
01:03:16,709 --> 01:03:17,684
Seven files.

643
01:03:18,634 --> 01:03:22,628
Step five: read exactly those files with read parquet, and count.

644
01:03:22,908 --> 01:03:26,293
Twelve million six hundred ninety-five thousand eight hundred three.

645
01:03:27,243 --> 01:03:30,520
And the same count, the normal way, from raw transactions.

646
01:03:30,800 --> 01:03:31,574
Identical.

647
01:03:31,854 --> 01:03:34,551
The lake is nothing but these tables and these files.

648
01:03:35,501 --> 01:03:40,587
With deletes, one more step: read the delete files too, and skip the positions they list.

649
01:03:41,537 --> 01:03:47,671
And that is all any engine needs to read DuckLake: some SQL against the catalog, and a Parquet reader.

650
01:03:47,951 --> 01:03:48,988
Nothing is hidden.

651
01:03:49,938 --> 01:03:55,141
Save the queries as 5 underscore 3 read by hand dot sequel, commit, and push.

652
01:04:17,668 --> 01:04:21,962
To recap: snapshot, table, data path, valid files, read parquet.

653
01:04:22,242 --> 01:04:23,650
Same answer as the lake.

654
01:04:23,930 --> 01:04:25,405
That ends Module 5.

655
01:04:25,685 --> 01:04:28,124
Next, we put dbt on top of the lake.

656
01:04:36,089 --> 01:04:39,572
In this module, dbt builds models on top of our lake.

657
01:04:39,852 --> 01:04:44,493
It reads the raw tables, and writes its models into the lake too, in the schema main.

658
01:04:45,443 --> 01:04:50,022
dbt runs its own DuckDB, inside dbt duckdb.

659
01:04:50,302 --> 01:04:52,194
So it needs to attach the lake itself.

660
01:04:52,474 --> 01:04:55,096
That is one change, in profiles dot yml.

661
01:04:56,046 --> 01:04:59,252
In DBT Studio, open profiles dot yml.

662
01:04:59,532 --> 01:05:03,010
Today it points at the small dev database from Lesson 2.1.

663
01:05:03,960 --> 01:05:05,160
Replace the output.

664
01:05:05,440 --> 01:05:09,746
Path is a small local DuckDB file for dbt's own session.

665
01:05:10,026 --> 01:05:14,549
Attach adds the lake: the ducklake prefix and the catalog file, as eth lake.

666
01:05:14,829 --> 01:05:19,015
Database eth lake makes the lake dbt's target, in schema main.

667
01:05:19,295 --> 01:05:27,911
And one thread, the safe choice with a single-file catalog: several dbt threads writing to one DuckLake can still trip over each other.

668
01:05:28,861 --> 01:05:38,351
Check it: in the command box under the terminal, run dbt debug. dbt finds the project and the profile, attaches the lake, and connects.

669
01:05:38,631 --> 01:05:39,725
All checks passed.

670
01:05:41,948 --> 01:05:44,757
Commit the profile: point dbt at the lake.

671
01:05:45,037 --> 01:05:45,744
And push.

672
01:05:59,112 --> 01:06:00,769
Now the rule for this module.

673
01:06:01,050 --> 01:06:03,591
Open Data Lake, and the eth lake instance.

674
01:06:03,871 --> 01:06:06,318
Studio opens the lake, and keeps it open.

675
01:06:07,268 --> 01:06:10,889
Back in DBT Studio, run dbt debug again.

676
01:06:11,169 --> 01:06:15,786
This time it fails: conflicting lock is held in Rosetta DBT Studio.

677
01:06:16,066 --> 01:06:20,132
A DuckDB catalog file can be opened by one program at a time.

678
01:06:23,530 --> 01:06:30,718
So in this module, we always run dbt first, and open the lake in Studio afterwards, to look at the result.

679
01:06:31,668 --> 01:06:34,120
Studio keeps the lake open until you quit it.

680
01:06:34,400 --> 01:06:39,487
To run dbt again after looking at the lake, quit Studio, and open it again.

681
01:06:39,767 --> 01:06:42,955
With a PostgreSQL catalog, this limit goes away.

682
01:06:43,905 --> 01:06:51,645
To recap: profiles dot yml attaches the lake and makes it dbt's database, and dbt debug passes.

683
01:06:51,925 --> 01:06:55,809
One program at a time: dbt first, then the lake screens.

684
01:06:56,089 --> 01:06:57,616
Next: staging models.

685
01:07:05,566 --> 01:07:09,711
In DBT Studio, in the models folder, create sources dot yml.

686
01:07:09,991 --> 01:07:16,852
A source called raw, in database eth lake, schema raw, with the two tables we loaded: blocks and transactions.

687
01:07:17,802 --> 01:07:22,262
Make a folder models, staging, and in it stg blocks dot sequel.

688
01:07:22,542 --> 01:07:29,259
A view that renames the columns clearly, computes gas used in percent, and turns the base fee from wei into gwei.

689
01:07:34,223 --> 01:07:41,470
And stg transactions dot sequel: the value and the fee paid in ether, and a success flag from the receipt status.

690
01:07:44,668 --> 01:07:48,117
In the command box, run dbt run, select staging.

691
01:07:48,397 --> 01:07:50,673
Two views, created in the lake, in a second.

692
01:07:56,272 --> 01:08:02,891
Look at some rows with dbt show: each transaction with its block, date, addresses, and value in ether.

693
01:08:03,171 --> 01:08:08,135
The terminal shows the columns that fit; the fee and the success flag are further right.

694
01:08:09,085 --> 01:08:15,979
A view in a DuckLake is only a row in the catalog, in ducklake view: its SQL text, and no data files at all.

695
01:08:16,259 --> 01:08:19,993
So staging views cost nothing to keep, and are always up to date.

696
01:08:20,943 --> 01:08:24,444
dbt is done, so now we can open the lake in Studio.

697
01:08:24,724 --> 01:08:30,602
In Data Lake, eth lake, refresh: the two staging views are listed, in schema main, with type view.

698
01:08:31,552 --> 01:08:34,262
Commit the source and the staging models, and push.

699
01:08:49,327 --> 01:08:57,946
To recap: sources point dbt at the raw tables, two staging views clean them up, and in a lake a view is just a row in the catalog.

700
01:08:58,226 --> 01:09:01,276
Next: marts, built incrementally, with a real merge.

701
01:09:09,233 --> 01:09:17,696
In the marts folder, daily network activity: per day, the number of blocks and transactions, how full the blocks were, and the average base fee.

702
01:09:17,976 --> 01:09:18,630
A table.

703
01:09:21,945 --> 01:09:27,426
And top senders: the hundred addresses that sent the most transactions, with the fees they paid.

704
01:09:32,063 --> 01:09:37,240
Now daily gas, incremental, with the merge strategy and block date as the unique key.

705
01:09:37,520 --> 01:09:42,378
On later runs it only processes days from the last one it has, and merges them in.

706
01:09:42,658 --> 01:09:48,602
The post hook calls set commit message, so the snapshots daily gas makes get an author and a message.

707
01:09:49,552 --> 01:09:51,572
Run everything: dbt run.

708
01:09:51,852 --> 01:09:55,626
Two views, two tables, and the incremental model, built in the lake.

709
01:10:00,016 --> 01:10:01,275
Run daily gas again.

710
01:10:01,555 --> 01:10:09,195
This time it is incremental: dbt recomputes the last day into a temporary table, and merges it into daily gas.

711
01:10:12,203 --> 01:10:18,487
Here is what dbt ran, in the target folder: merge into eth lake dot main dot daily gas.

712
01:10:18,767 --> 01:10:23,837
The merge Studio's SQL Editor could not run in Lesson 3.2, running on our lake.

713
01:10:24,787 --> 01:10:29,846
And the result, with dbt show: one row per day, from August to early November.

714
01:10:35,294 --> 01:10:43,266
dbt is done, so open the lake in Studio: Data Lake, eth lake, History. dbt's work is a series of snapshots.

715
01:10:43,546 --> 01:10:47,825
And the incremental merge has an author, dbt, and our commit message.

716
01:10:48,775 --> 01:10:50,108
Commit the marts, and push.

717
01:11:06,670 --> 01:11:13,482
To recap: two table marts, and an incremental model whose merge strategy runs a real merge into on the lake.

718
01:11:13,762 --> 01:11:18,796
A post hook adds a commit message, and every dbt run shows up as snapshots.

719
01:11:19,076 --> 01:11:20,606
Next: tests and docs.

720
01:11:28,566 --> 01:11:34,499
A DuckLake supports not null, but no primary keys, no unique constraints, and no foreign keys.

721
01:11:34,779 --> 01:11:37,310
Nothing stops a duplicate block from getting in.

722
01:11:38,260 --> 01:11:42,215
So we check those rules with dbt tests, every time we build.

723
01:11:43,165 --> 01:11:45,331
In models, create schema dot yml.

724
01:11:45,611 --> 01:11:57,524
Descriptions for the models, unique and not null on the keys, accepted values on the success flag, and a relationship: every transaction's block number must exist in stg blocks.

725
01:11:58,474 --> 01:12:04,556
Run dbt build: it builds every model in order, and runs each model's tests right after it.

726
01:12:04,836 --> 01:12:08,259
Five models and nine tests: fourteen passes in all.

727
01:12:12,793 --> 01:12:13,731
Now the docs.

728
01:12:14,011 --> 01:12:21,344
Project menu, Generate Docs. dbt reads the project and the lake, and writes a small website into target.

729
01:12:23,903 --> 01:12:25,470
Then Project, Serve Docs.

730
01:12:25,750 --> 01:12:28,922
Studio starts a local web server, on port eighty eighty.

731
01:12:31,195 --> 01:12:35,976
Open it in the browser, on the daily gas model: its description, and its columns.

732
01:12:36,926 --> 01:12:44,992
And the lineage graph for this model: daily gas is built from stg transactions, the staging view on the raw transactions table.

733
01:12:45,271 --> 01:12:51,210
The full graph of the project, from both raw tables to all three marts, opens with the button at the top.

734
01:12:52,160 --> 01:12:54,459
Commit the tests and descriptions, and push.

735
01:13:06,818 --> 01:13:14,623
To recap: the lake does not enforce unique keys, so dbt tests do, and dbt build runs them with every model.

736
01:13:14,903 --> 01:13:18,791
And the docs show each model, its columns and tests, and its lineage.

737
01:13:19,071 --> 01:13:20,516
That ends Module 6.

738
01:13:20,796 --> 01:13:22,961
One lesson left: where to go from here.

739
01:13:30,911 --> 01:13:35,989
Our lake lives on a laptop: a DuckDB file as catalog, and a local folder.

740
01:13:36,269 --> 01:13:37,762
For a team, swap both.

741
01:13:38,042 --> 01:13:43,244
Use PostgreSQL as the catalog, and object storage like S3 for the data.

742
01:13:43,524 --> 01:13:45,370
Same lake format, same SQL.

743
01:13:46,320 --> 01:13:51,264
Now many people and many machines can attach the same lake, and write at the same time.

744
01:13:52,214 --> 01:14:00,344
We tested S3 compatible storage too: it works from DuckDB and dbt, but Studio's wizard does not support it yet.

745
01:14:00,624 --> 01:14:04,719
A follow-up tutorial covers PostgreSQL with cloud storage.

746
01:14:05,669 --> 01:14:08,286
Running a lake for real means a few more things.

747
01:14:08,566 --> 01:14:14,902
Access control comes from two places: permissions in the catalog database, and policies on the bucket.

748
01:14:15,852 --> 01:14:20,033
Backups: back up the catalog database, and turn on versioning for the bucket.

749
01:14:20,983 --> 01:14:25,900
Encryption: DuckLake can encrypt every data file, and keeps the keys in the catalog.

750
01:14:26,850 --> 01:14:31,729
And an existing DuckDB database can be moved into a DuckLake, table by table.

751
01:14:32,679 --> 01:14:38,724
Some things are not supported today, like primary keys, unique and foreign key constraints, and indexes.

752
01:14:39,004 --> 01:14:43,546
Check the docs page on unsupported features: it changes with each release.

753
01:14:44,496 --> 01:14:45,431
Let's look back.

754
01:14:45,711 --> 01:14:49,048
A DuckLake is a catalog database plus Parquet files.

755
01:14:49,328 --> 01:14:52,145
Every change is a snapshot you can travel back to.

756
01:14:52,425 --> 01:14:54,729
Updates and deletes never rewrite files.

757
01:14:55,009 --> 01:14:58,602
Partitions, statistics and maintenance keep it fast and small.

758
01:14:58,882 --> 01:15:01,855
The catalog is plain SQL you can read yourself.

759
01:15:02,135 --> 01:15:05,113
And dbt builds tested models right on top.

760
01:15:05,393 --> 01:15:07,984
Thanks for watching, and see you in the next course.
